Subject: [PATCH 3/3] FatPartition: sync the FAT cache before the data cache

With USE_SEPARATE_FAT_CACHE, cacheSync() wrote the data cache (which
holds the directory sector a file sync just updated) before the FAT
cache. A reset between the two left a directory entry pointing at
clusters the FAT still marks free; the next allocation reused them and
two files shared data. Writing the FAT first, as the shared cache
already did, means an interrupted allocation can only leak clusters,
which fsck reclaims safely.

This order makes freeing unsafe instead. remove(), truncate() to zero
and O_TRUNC free clusters in the FAT before the directory entry
changes. With the old order, a reset during a free that fit in one FAT
sector only leaked clusters. Now a reset after the FAT write and before
the directory write can leave an entry pointing at free clusters, as
with the shared cache.

---
--- a/src/FatLib/FatPartition.h
+++ b/src/FatLib/FatPartition.h
@@ -203,7 +203,9 @@
     return m_fatCache.prepare(sector, options);
   }
   bool cacheSync() {
-    return m_cache.sync() && m_fatCache.sync() && syncDevice();
+    // FAT first: an interrupted allocation only leaks clusters, but an
+    // interrupted free (remove, truncate, O_TRUNC) can leave a dangling entry.
+    return m_fatCache.sync() && m_cache.sync() && syncDevice();
   }
 #else   // USE_SEPARATE_FAT_CACHE
   uint8_t* fatCachePrepare(Sector_t sector, uint8_t options) {
